  "fx.readme.none": "- (no effects)",
  "fx.readme.body": "# AstraForge effects for Godot 4.5\n\nThis archive was built in the AstraForge studio (Effects tab → Export for Godot). The effect format is `astraforge-fx/1`, the same as in the Forge:\nGPUParticles3D + ParticleProcessMaterial + QuadMesh (+ OmniLight3D if the effect has its light on). Each effect emits along its local +Y axis.\n\n## Contents\n\n{list}\n\n- `fx/textures/`: particle textures (PNG). Paths in the .tscn files are relative, so move the `fx/` folder as a whole.\n- `fx_bindings.json`: where each effect sits on the model: effect name (`name`), scene (`scene`), parent (`parent`, an object or a bone), `node` (name of the effect point in the GLB), `transform` (local position, rotation and scale relative to the parent).\n- `af_fx_mount.gd`: the AstraForge mount script: finds model nodes by name and instances the effect scenes in them.\n\n## How to hook it up\n\n1. Unpack the archive into the root of your Godot project (the `fx/` folder and two files next to it).\n2. Export the model from the studio (Export → GLB) and add it to the project. Each effect in the GLB is an empty node named after the `node` field; it already sits at the right point and carries the effect parameters in `extras.fx`.\n   Godot turns a bone with such a child node into a BoneAttachment3D, so the effect follows the bone.\n3. Create a wrapper scene: a Node3D root with an inheriting script (below) and a child node `model`, an instance of the GLB.\n4. The inheriting script reads `fx_bindings.json` and hands the list of bindings to `af_fx_mount.gd` before its `_ready`:\n\n```gdscript\nextends \"res://af_fx_mount.gd\"\n\nfunc _init() -> void:\n    var data = JSON.parse_string(FileAccess.get_file_as_string(\"res://fx_bindings.json\"))\n    for b in data:\n        fx_bindings.append({\n            \"socket\": b[\"node_godot\"],              # effect point in the GLB: position and rotation are already inside the model\n            \"scene\": load(\"res://\" + b[\"scene\"]),\n            \"name\": b[\"name\"],\n        })\n```\n\n   `af_fx_mount.gd` then finds the nodes by name and instances the scenes in them. One-shot effects are restarted with `trigger(\"name\")`.\n\n## If the model is not from the studio\n\nUse `parent` instead of `node` and place the effect yourself, applying `transform` from `fx_bindings.json`:\n\n```gdscript\nvar fx := (load(\"res://\" + b[\"scene\"]) as PackedScene).instantiate() as Node3D\nparent_node.add_child(fx)\nvar t = b[\"transform\"]\nfx.position = Vector3(t[\"position\"][0], t[\"position\"][1], t[\"position\"][2])\nfx.quaternion = Quaternion(t[\"quaternion\"][0], t[\"quaternion\"][1], t[\"quaternion\"][2], t[\"quaternion\"][3])\n```\n\n## Notes\n\n- Effect sizes are in metres. The emitter scale set in the studio (Scale tool) is already baked into the scene parameters, like `scale` of the Forge effects.\n- One-shot effects (`one_shot`) start on `emitting = true` or `restart()`; `af_fx_mount.gd` provides `trigger(\"name\")`.\n- An effect with `local_coords = false` leaves a trail in the world; with `true` it moves with its node.\n- Texture frame animation (flipbook) is set up in the material: `particles_anim_h_frames` / `particles_anim_v_frames`.\n",
